'''Sartisanian''' ({{native name|ca|Sartisà|paren=omit}}) is a mixed language consisting of [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] with substantial [[Sartan language|Sartan]] vocabulary, spoken by a small minority of [[Sartan Balboan people|Sartan Balboans]] in [[Balboa]]. Because Sartan and Volisanian are closely related {{w|Romance languages|Mesogean Romance languages}} sharing a common Latin-derived origin, Sartisanian is not a full creole or contact language but rather a lexical blending: Volisanian grammar and phonology with Sartan words substituted for common terms, particularly in domains relating to food, family, religion, and rural life.

The language developed principally in the nineteenth century among Sartan immigrants who lived and worked in Volisanian-speaking neighbourhoods in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and [[Sant Bart]]. It is estimated that fewer than 5 per cent of Sartan Balboans have functional proficiency, though passive recognition of Sartan-derived vocabulary is more widespread within the community.

== Classification ==

Sartisanian is classified as a mixed language of the Mesogean Romance family. Its grammatical structure, phonology, and syntax are overwhelmingly Volisanian, while its lexicon draws substantially from Sartan. The two parent languages — Volisanian and Sartan — are themselves closely related, both descending from {{w|Vulgar Latin}} and sharing a high degree of mutual intelligibility. Sartisanian exploits this closeness: the substitution of Sartan words into Volisanian sentence structures produces a variety that is largely intelligible to Volisanian speakers, though immediately recognisable as distinct.

The language is not a creole. Unlike [[Balboan Creole]], which emerged from contact between Volisanian-speaking colonists and Kaftian-speaking enslaved populations and developed a distinct grammar, Sartisanian has no substrate influence from an unrelated language family. Its structure remains Volisanian throughout.

== History ==

Sartisanian emerged in the nineteenth century in the urban centres of Balboa, where Sartan immigrants — principally civil war refugees and, later, Redilution-era farmers who had moved to the cities — lived alongside the dominant Volisanian-speaking white Balboan population. The close relationship between Sartan and Volisanian facilitated lexical mixing: Sartan immigrants could substitute Sartan words into Volisanian conversation without disrupting intelligibility, and over time a stable set of Sartan-derived lexical substitutions became conventionalised within Sartan Balboan households and neighbourhoods.

The Guajicutea-originated Sartans, who arrived in 1824 following the [[Virtuous Revolution]] in [[Guajicutea]], were among the earliest Sartan speakers to live alongside Volisanian-speaking communities in Balboa and contributed to the bilingual environment from which Sartisanian emerged.

The language was never standardised and has no literary tradition. It has been transmitted orally within families and community networks, and its use has declined steadily across generations as Sartan Balboans have assimilated into the Volisanian-speaking majority.

== Present status ==

Sartisanian has no official status in Balboa and is not recognised under the [[Twelve Guarantees]] or any language protection legislation. The language is not used in education, government, or media. Its use is confined to domestic and community settings, primarily in the urban centres of [[Sant Cristòfor]] and [[Sant Bart]] and in a small number of rural communities in [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] where Sartan Balboan concentration is highest.

The number of fluent speakers is estimated at approximately 30,000, though passive knowledge of Sartan-derived vocabulary is more widespread within the Sartan Balboan community. The language is in decline, as younger generations of Sartan Balboans are monolingual in Volisanian and have no functional knowledge of Sartan or Sartisanian. No language revitalisation movement exists.

